Following the measured data for $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decays, we conduct a shape analysis of the differential decay rate, focusing on both the $B_s^0$ and $D_s^{*-}$ meson polarizations. Comparing various possible mass form factor expressions, we perform a shape fit using the available data. Our results indicate that the "helicity" and "consensus" forms are suitable for shape analysis of $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay, with a confidence level of 67% and 81%, respectively, based on the current data. This implies that all three form factor constants are necessary to fully describe the form of the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay. In contrast, the "axial" and "simple" models, which assume a reduction in dimension from 3 to 2, are insufficient, with confidence levels of 17% and 24%, respectively. This calls for further precision in measurements to more accurately determine the form factor parameters and potentially uncover new physics insights.
